__d(function(e,o,n,t,a,r,i){a.exports={title:"The Michigan Dogman",eyebrow:"Legend",intro:"The Dogman belongs to northern Michigan campfire culture: a tall figure with canine features and an unsettling howl, tied to logging camps, lonely roads, and deep woods. Treat it as folklore \u2014 a mirror for how people describe fear, strangeness, and the north woods after dark.",sections:[{heading:"Early reports and geography",body:"Popular accounts often cite an 1887 story from a logging camp along the Manistee River in Wexford County, northwest of Clare County \u2014 placing the tale in the same pine-and-river country many residents still know. Whether or not one credits a single sighting, the narrative has traveled along rivers and county lines for more than a century.",lore_callout:"The legend holds in some tellings that the creature appears on the seventh year of every decade \u2014 a detail that belongs to story, not science."},{heading:"1987 and the modern legend",body:"Traverse City radio host Steve Cook aired a song about the Dogman as an April Fools' piece in 1987. Listeners called in with their own accounts. The bit became a long-running piece of Michigan popular culture and helped standardize the story for new generations.",lore_callout:"That broadcast is a documented cultural moment; it does not prove zoology."},{heading:"Reading the legend responsibly",body:"Some writers connect Dogman stories to Odawa sacred geography along the Manistee or to broader shape-shifter themes. Those connections are interpretive. On unicorn.gives we share the lore as part of regional identity \u2014 never as a substitute for wildlife biology or public safety information."}],seo_description:"Michigan Dogman folklore, northern Michigan legend, and cultural context for Clare County and the Land of the Unicorns."}},1007,[],"data/lore/dogman.json");
__d(function(e,i,t,n,a,s,r){a.exports={title:"The Memegwesiwak",eyebrow:"River tradition",intro:"Memegwesiwak (singular Memegwesi) are riverbank spirits in Anishinaabe narrative \u2014 small beings tied to currents, stones, and the etiquette of traveling water. They appear in teaching stories about humility, offerings, and paying attention when the canoe drifts sideways on a calm day.",sections:[{heading:"Who they are in story",body:"Descriptions emphasize river places: child-sized figures, voices compared to insects, stone markings, and small stone canoes. Stories warn against disrespect \u2014 nets tugged, boats nudged \u2014 and encourage tobacco, food, or small gifts as acknowledgment in traditional telling.",lore_callout:"These are teachings and narratives within Indigenous traditions, not tourist characters."},{heading:"European observers",body:"French observers in the 1600s recorded Ojibwe paddlers tossing tobacco toward certain river rocks \u2014 interpreted by outsiders as jest or superstition. Such notes are colonial glimpses; they are not authoritative explanations of Anishinaabe belief."},{heading:"This landscape",body:"Clare County's inland lakes and rivers \u2014 including the Muskegon River to the west and the Tobacco River in the east \u2014 sit in the kind of water-rich country where such stories have been told for generations. unicorn.gives names them to honor the depth of place, not to flatten living cultures into spectacle."}],seo_description:"Memegwesiwak river spirits in Anishinaabe tradition and northern Michigan waterways context."}},1008,[],"data/lore/memegwesiwak.json");
__d(function(e,a,i,t,n,r,o){n.exports={title:"Mishibizhiw \u2014 the underwater lynx",eyebrow:"Great Lakes cosmology",intro:"Mishibizhiw is a powerful underwater being in Ojibwe narrative \u2014 often described with feline and serpent features, associated with the depths, storms, and the moral seriousness of crossing big water. Thunderers appear as opposing powers in many tellings.",sections:[{heading:"Guardians of depth",body:"Significant lakes and rivers were understood to have spiritual as well as physical dangers. Offerings and careful behavior before travel reflect a worldview in which water demands respect \u2014 a teaching that still resonates alongside modern boating and ice safety.",lore_callout:"Stories about Mishibizhiw belong to Indigenous knowledge systems; retell them with attribution and care."},{heading:"Thunder and weather",body:"Storms could be narrated as struggle between thunder beings and underwater powers. That is mythic language for power and unpredictability \u2014 not a meteorology textbook, but part of how people made moral and emotional sense of the sky and lakes."},{heading:"Clare County lakes",body:"Many local lakes punch above their size in depth and cold. The civic map lists them for recreation and property; the lore layer remembers that, for Ojibwe communities, those waters were never only amenities \u2014 they were (and are) relatives and responsibilities in story and law."}],seo_description:"Mishibizhiw underwater panther in Ojibwe tradition and connection to northern Michigan lakes."}},1009,[],"data/lore/mishibizhiw.json");
__d(function(e,n,a,i,t,o,s){t.exports={title:"Anishinaabe roots on this land",eyebrow:"Land and treaties",intro:"Anishinaabeg \u2014 including Ojibwe, Odawa, and Potawatomi nations \u2014 have deep histories in the Great Lakes region. Clare County sits within ceded territory addressed by treaties between the United States and Indigenous nations. A civic app should tell that truth clearly and point residents toward learning more.",sections:[{heading:"Council of Three Fires",body:"The Three Fires Confederacy links Ojibwe, Odawa, and Potawatomi peoples in a long political and kinship tradition. Each community has its own governance and history; umbrella terms are useful for orientation but should not erase specific nations, bands, and treaties.",lore_callout:"Use nation-specific names when you can; defer to tribal sources for teaching and policy."},{heading:"1836 and after",body:"The Treaty of Washington (1836) is among the agreements through which the United States claimed large areas of Michigan's lower peninsula. Later agreements and federal policy reshaped reserved lands, removal, and recognition. The legal landscape is complex; summaries here are introductions, not legal advice."},{heading:"Why this belongs in civic tech",body:"Land acknowledgment on a county platform is not decoration. It frames who has standing, whose treaties still govern resource and co-management questions, and why 'Up North' was never empty wilderness. unicorn.gives aims to be plainspoken, warm, and accurate \u2014 the opposite of erasure."}],seo_description:"Anishinaabe history, treaties, and land acknowledgment context for Clare County and unicorn.gives."}},1010,[],"data/lore/anishinaabe.json");
